{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VWPAqRtjhe4ErDkhKcGdsQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. It derives formal specifications from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Shared output formatting\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n\n- `docs/` - Specifications\n\n**Current State of Implementation:**\nLooking at what's already been built, I can see the testing infrastructure is in place with differential testing and normalization utilities, the system abstraction layer handles platform-specific file info and terminal operations, the formatting package provides color and column output with size conversions, and there's a basic cat implementation started in the cmd directory.\n\nThe command implementations are partially there across cat, du, ls, sponge, wc, and version, each with main files and test files already present.\n\n**Releases and their status:**\nThe foundational infrastructure in rel00.0 shows as not started in the analysis, but the source code clearly has implementations for the test utilities, system layer, formatting, and version command already done. The cat command in rel01.1 appears to be underway with both main and test files.\n\nLooking at the remaining batches, rel01.2 through rel01.3 follow the same pattern—sponge and du both have code despite being marked as not started in the analysis. The later releases like rel02.0 through rel03.0 are still pending on the spec side with no code started yet.\n\nBut I need to reconsider what's actually missing here. The constraint is clear: if source code exists for a package, it already exists and shouldn't be reimplemented. Let me cross-reference what's actually in the codebase against what the roadmap is calling for to see what truly needs to be built versus what's already there. I see there are orphaned PRDs for true and false that exist but aren't properly connected to the roadmap structure. The earliest incomplete release is rel02.0, but since its spec status is \"pending,\" I need to check whether those orphaned PRDs could actually fulfill the requirement, or if I'm limited to proposing tasks only for releases that already have complete specifications. I see that rel02.0, rel02.1, and rel02.2 all have pending spec status, which means I can't write code tasks without PRDs. But I notice there are orphaned PRDs for true and false—prd012-true and prd013-false—so some specification work has already been done for those. Since true and false are the simplest utilities to implement, I could propose using those existing specs as a starting point, even though the formal status is still pending. these PRDs are already written but just not properly linked in the roadmap?\n\nGiven the project workflow (specs before code), and that rel02.0 has pending spec status, I need to look at what the earliest actionable work is.\n\nSince I can't read files (constraint: \"Do NOT use any tools\"), I need to work with what I have.\n\nThe orphaned PRDs prd012-true and prd013-false exist. The spec status for rel02.0 is \"pending\" but there ARE orphaned PRDs. \n\nFor the 1 task I can propose, I should focus on the earliest incomplete release (rel02.0) and the simplest possible task. \n\nSince true and false have orphaned PRDs (prd012-true, prd013-false), and these are the most trivial utilities possible, I should propose implementing one of them.\n\n`true` is the simplest utility - it just exits with code 0, while `false` exits with code 1. These are about as basic as it gets.\n\nI'll propose implementing cmd/true based on prd012-true. The implementation needs to handle exit code 0, accept and ignore any arguments, and deal with SIGPIPE - that's really all there is to it.\n\nSince I don't have the PRD content in the project context, I can reference prd012-true in required_reading and the stitch agent will have access to it when they read the task.\n\nWriting the Go implementation... Looking at the roadmap status, I see that `true` and `false` are next in line, but they're so minimal that combining them into a single task makes sense given the constraint of one task maximum. The code status shows both are pending, and while they're trivial utilities, grouping them would still fit within the four sub-requirements limit. Looking at the requirements for both commands, they're minimal enough that combining them might exceed the 4 sub-requirement limit if each PRD has 2-3 requirements. I'll play it safe and propose implementing just `cmd/true` first, since it has a PRD and fits the project's sequential pattern.\n\nThe `true` command is essentially just returning exit code 0, with optional help and version flags. Given how trivial the implementation would be, I think combining the implementation and tests into a single task makes sense here—the planning guidelines say to combine trivial changes, and splitting this would be unnecessarily fragmented. The total code would be under 250 lines anyway.\n\nBut wait, the constitution explicitly forbids implementing and testing the same feature in one task. That's a hard constraint I need to follow. So I'd have to propose just the implementation without tests, but that leaves me with maybe 15-25 lines of code, which is way below the 150-250 line target.\n\nWhat if I combine `true` and `false` into a single implementation task? They follow the same pattern—one exits with 0, the other with 1—and together they'd hit a more reasonable line count.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the constraints more carefully. The task limit is 1 task, and I need 150-250 lines of production code. Even combining both utilities, I'm looking at only 30-50 lines. That's still short, but at least it groups trivial changes together as the constitution suggests.\n\nThere's also the sub-requirement limit: each task can have at most 4 PRD sub-requirements. If I combine two PRDs, I need to check both, but for simple utilities like these, each PRD probably only has 2-3 sub-requirements anyway.\n\nI think I'll go with implementing `cmd/true` alone. It's the first use case in the earliest incomplete release, and even though it's small, it establishes the pattern for the rest of the batch 2 trivial utilities. But I need to make sure the task is independently executable—an agent should be able to complete it with just the task description and no additional context.\n\nThat means I need to reference the PRD in the required_reading section. Let me also check what GNU `true` actually does: it exits with status 0, accepts `--help` and `--version`, and ignores everything else. Looking at the existing patterns in the codebase, I'll need the copyright header, SPDX license, package comment referencing the PRD, SIGPIPE handling, and flag parsing. Now I'm drafting the task structure with the proper YAML format and description. I'm laying out the design approach: I'll follow the SIGPIPE handling pattern from the version utility, use flag.Parse to consume all flags without processing them, and structure it as a standalone binary in the cmd/true directory. The acceptance criteria cover compilation, exit codes for various argument scenarios, and copyright headers.\n\nNow I'm realizing I don't actually have the specific requirement IDs from prd012-true, so I should reference the PRD generically and let the stitch agent pull the exact requirement structure. The constraint requires anchoring tasks to specific PRD IDs, but since I'm working with a PRD I haven't seen, I'll use the PRD number itself and assume a standard requirement structure like R1.1 for exit status and R1.2 for argument handling.\n\nActually, I'm noticing that `true` and `false` are trivial enough to combine into a single task—together they'd be minimal code—which aligns with the planning principle of consolidating simple changes. I need to make sure the task title captures which PRD requirements this covers, even if I'm being general about the specific IDs.\n\nLet me reconsider: I can only propose one task, so combining `true` and `false` makes sense for maximizing value. Both utilities are in the same release and each probably has just a couple of requirements, so I should stay well under the 4 sub-requirement limit.\n\nI'm drafting a single task that implements both utilities together, referencing their respective PRDs and the architecture patterns. The task includes creating the main.go files for each command—`true` exits with 0, `false` exits with 1—and I'm starting to outline the requirements section. I'm using flag.Parse() to consume any command-line arguments without actually defining flags, which matches the standard GNU behavior for these utilities. Now I'm looking at the acceptance criteria to ensure both binaries compile correctly, exit with the right status codes, handle arbitrary arguments properly, and include the required documentation headers and package comments.\n\nWait, but I need to reconsider the PRD structure here. The requirements span two separate PRDs—prd012-true and prd013-false—with R3 and R4 being cross-cutting concerns that apply to both. Looking at the sub-requirements, I'm covering prd012-true R1.1 (exit 0) and prd013-false R1.1 (exit 1) plus the SIGPIPE handling, which stays within the 4 sub-requirement limit.\n\nI should make sure the stitch agent reads the actual PRD contents first since I'm not entirely certain what they contain. Combining the true and false cases makes sense—they're both in rel02.0, they're the simplest Unix utilities, the combined code is still well under limits, the planning constitution supports combining trivial changes, and both have orphaned PRDs that need implementation. But I'm uncertain whether \"orphaned\" means the PRDs exist but aren't linked to use cases, or something else entirely. Let me finalize the proposal with that caveat in mind.\n\nActually, the consistency check confirms they're orphaned PRDs, which means the files definitely exist—they're just not properly referenced in the roadmap's use case structure. That's workable since the stitch agent can read them directly. I'm going ahead with the combined true and false task.\n\nOne concern: my draft has 4 requirements, 6 acceptance criteria, and 3 design decisions. The requirements fall slightly below the 5-8 target, but artificially inflating them for trivial utilities would violate the constitution's guidance against splitting single requirements into sub-bullets. For something this simple, 4 requirements feels appropriate.\n\nFinalizing the task definition with the YAML structure... I'm setting up signal handling for both utilities following the pattern from cmd/version, where SIGPIPE triggers an exit with code 0 for true and 1 for false. Each binary will be a standalone package under cmd/ that calls flag.Parse() to silently consume any flags or arguments, matching GNU coreutils behavior. The acceptance criteria verify that both compile successfully and produce the correct exit codes.\n\nWait, I need to reconsider D3—GNU true actually accepts --help and --version flags but ignores everything else, rather than consuming all flags indiscriminately. The core behavior is just returning 0 with no side effects.\n\nLooking at how the existing utilities handle this, they all use flag.Parse() with defined flags, but for true and false the simplest approach is to skip flag parsing entirely and just exit immediately with the appropriate code, since GNU true's fundamental behavior is to do nothing and succeed.\n\nI should be careful not to invent design decisions beyond what the architecture and PRD actually specify. The SIGPIPE pattern from cmd/version and the package naming convention are solid constraints, but I'm overspeculating on flag handling without having read the actual requirements. Let me stick to what I can derive from the existing codebase and remove the invented details about how to handle flags.\n\nFor SIGPIPE behavior, all the existing utilities exit 0 when encountering a broken pipe, which makes sense since SIGPIPE is a signal condition, not a failure of the utility itself. Even though false's contract is to exit 1, that's about its normal exit code—SIGPIPE handling is orthogonal to that. I'll let the stitch agent work from the PRD to make that call rather than prescribing it myself.\n\nSince I can't access the specific sub-requirement IDs within the PRDs, I'll reference them at the top level (prd012-true and prd013-false) and trust the agent to read the full requirements and implement accordingly. Now I'm ready to finalize the proposal.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":89760,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":89760},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a6c27bb-a9f6-4c7d-b475-946d9d91ebdf","uuid":"13f3500f-6280-4914-b356-1f163fa3af57"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VWPAqRtjhe4ErDkhKcGdsQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven code generation experiment. It derives formal specs from C source code of classic Unix utilities, generates Go implementations via an AI orchestrator, and verifies parity against GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with shared infrastructure in `pkg/` (testutils, sys, format) and per-utility packages in `cmd/`. Magefiles orchestrate the build/generation cycle.\n\n**Current State:** All foundational packages (`pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`) and six utilities (`cmd/cat`, `cmd/du`, `cmd/ls`, `cmd/sponge`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/version`) have source code. Releases 00.0, 01.1, 01.2, 01.3, 03.0, 04.0, and 04.1 are implemented.\n\n**Next Release:** rel02.0 (Batch 2 — trivial utilities: true, false, yes, basename, dirname) is the earliest incomplete release. Orphaned PRDs exist for `true` (prd012-true) and `false` (prd013-false), making these the first actionable items. Spec status for the remaining rel02.0 utilities (yes, basename, dirname) is pending.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is rel02.0. Two of its five utilities (true, false) have existing PRDs (orphaned but present). These are the simplest Unix utilities possible — `true` exits 0, `false` exits 1 — making them ideal for combining into a single task per P2 (\"Combine trivial changes into one task\"). Implementation before tests per P8.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/true, cmd/false: implement prd012-true and prd013-false exit-code behavior\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd012-true.yaml (PRD for true utility)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd013-false.yaml (PRD for false utility)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ component convention and SIGPIPE handling pattern)\n      - cmd/version/main.go (reference implementation for trivial utility structure)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/true/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: true utility -- exits 0 unconditionally\n      - path: cmd/false/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: false utility -- exits 1 unconditionally\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement cmd/true per prd012-true: the binary exits with status 0 regardless of any arguments passed\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement cmd/false per prd013-false: the binary exits with status 1 regardless of any arguments passed\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Both binaries handle SIGPIPE by exiting cleanly, following the signal handling pattern established in cmd/version/main.go\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Both files include the standard copyright header, SPDX-License-Identifier MIT, and a package doc comment referencing the implemented PRD requirements\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the cmd/version/main.go SIGPIPE pattern: signal.Notify(sigpipe, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a goroutine that calls os.Exit(0) on receipt\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Each utility is a standalone package under cmd/ with a single main.go file, per ARCHITECTURE.yaml cmd/ component convention\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Read both PRDs completely before writing any code. The PRDs are the authoritative source for flag handling, exit codes, and edge case behavior. Do not deviate from PRD requirements\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/true/main.go compiles successfully with go build ./cmd/true/\n      - id: AC2\n        text: cmd/false/main.go compiles successfully with go build ./cmd/false/\n      - id: AC3\n        text: The true binary exits with code 0 when invoked with no arguments\n      - id: AC4\n        text: The false binary exits with code 1 when invoked with no arguments\n      - id: AC5\n        text: Both binaries exit with their respective exit codes when invoked with arbitrary arguments\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"Both files include copyright header, SPDX-License-Identifier MIT, and package doc comment referencing the PRD\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":89760,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":89760},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a6c27bb-a9f6-4c7d-b475-946d9d91ebdf","uuid":"cb86be2e-6705-46ed-a335-fb121a9aec04"}
